QEMU virgl: 2D resources are made as screens

What: virgl_cmd_create_resource_2d asks virglrenderer for RENDER_TARGET |
SCANOUT (1 << 1 | 1 << 18) instead of RENDER_TARGET only.
Why: in virgl mode the guest kernel makes a 2D resource only for dumb buffers:
the text console (fbcon), plymouth, and KMS dumb screens and cursors. The guest
memory budget (virgl-resource-memory-budget.patch) gives screens and cursors a
256 MB reserve past the budget so one app that fills it does not also stop the
desktop's next screen or cursor; it finds them by their SCANOUT, CURSOR or
DISPLAY_TARGET bind, and 2D resources had none of these. vrend uses SCANOUT only
for GBM buffers, which macOS does not have, so nothing else changes.
Upstream: not sent (the reserve is OmacVM's).
Tests: Tests/virgl/test-resource-budget.c checks the reserve with exactly this
bind (a SCANOUT|RENDER_TARGET screen fits past the budget up to the reserve, one
row more is refused); the build checks the patched source has the bind.
--- a/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
+++ b/hw/display/virtio-gpu-virgl.c
@@ -346,7 +346,14 @@
     args.handle = c2d.resource_id;
     args.target = 2;
     args.format = c2d.format;
-    args.bind = (1 << 1);
+    /*
+     * OmacVM: a 2D resource is a dumb buffer: a screen (fbcon, plymouth,
+     * KMS dumb buffers) or a cursor. Mark it as a scanout (VIRGL_BIND_SCANOUT,
+     * 1 << 18) next to RENDER_TARGET so virglrenderer's memory budget lets it
+     * use the display reserve; vrend uses this bind only for GBM, which macOS
+     * does not have.
+     */
+    args.bind = (1 << 1) | (1 << 18);
     args.width = c2d.width;
     args.height = c2d.height;
     args.depth = 1;
